# The hybrid planner's contract, held to by every host that has one.
#
# A `--- plan` case asks plan_hybrid rather than translate, and asserts four
# things no ordinary case can see: how the pipeline was CLASSIFIED (pure_sql,
# hybrid or pure_memory), which PHYSICAL sources the SQL side reads (`--- tables`,
# first-use order, one entry per physical source), the SQL prefix (`--- expect`,
# absent for a pure-memory plan), and that the caller's AST is the same tree
# after planning and physical optimisation as before. The runner also checks the
# continuation is present exactly when the classification says it is.
#
# These exist because the conformance suite could not see the findings of the
# cross-language review: JS and C++ classified `X = ORDERS; X .> TAKE(1)` as
# pure memory because they unwound the raw AST before normalising it, three
# hosts reported the SEL binding NAME where the fourth reported the table, and
# Lisp had no source-table field at all. All 801 language cases were green
# throughout. docs/interim/sel_cross_language_code_review_remediation_plan.md
# is the review; docs/SQL-TRANSLATION.md §12.1 is the contract these pin.

### name: plan.pure-sql.direct
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 1
===

### name: plan.pure-sql.through-helper-assignment
--- note
The whole of finding F1. A helper variable is inlined by stage 1, and the
planner must look at the tree stage 1 produces -- the one the translator will
be handed -- not at the raw `seq`, which is not a pipeline and so was
classified as pure memory by the two hosts that unwound it first.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
X = ORDERS; X .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 1
===

### name: plan.pure-sql.value-binding-is-inlined
--- note
Normalisation also inlines a `value` binding as a literal, so the prefix the
planner probes carries the constant the translator would have used.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "LIMIT": {"kind": "value", "value": "5", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["id"] > LIMIT) .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`id` > 5) LIMIT 1
===

### name: plan.pure-memory.non-normalisable-falls-back
--- note
A program stage 1 refuses is a program that cannot be pushed down, which is a
pure-memory plan and not an exception: the planner exists to answer "how much
of this can the database do", and "none of it" is an answer. Three hosts let
E_SQL_ASSIGN escape here; the continuation is the original program, and the
source tables are still reported from it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
A += 1; ORDERS .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.hybrid.unsupported-suffix
--- note
ABORT has no SQL spelling, so the MAP stays in memory and the TAKE goes to the
database. The prefix is rendered as a derived table because the continuation's
projection is pushed into it by the MAP fall-through: the `id` field is
translatable and is projected, the `note` field is not and is computed on the
rows that come back.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(1) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "note", ABORT("no")))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `_sub1`.`id` AS `id` FROM (SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 1) `_sub1`
===

### name: plan.hybrid.fallthrough-keeps-downstream-steps
--- note
The MAP fall-through: the pushable half of the RECORD is projected in SQL and
every step AFTER the MAP goes with it, so the TAKE lands in the statement even
though it is written after the step that stays in memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "tag", ABORT("x"))) .> TAKE(3)
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`id` AS `id` FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 3
===

### name: plan.hybrid.longest-prefix
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_DESC(_["id"]) .> TAKE(1) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "tag", ABORT("x")))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT "_sub1"."id" AS "id" FROM (SELECT "o".* FROM "orders" "o" ORDER BY "o"."id" DESC LIMIT 1) "_sub1"
===

### name: plan.pure-memory.bound-relation-in-source
--- note
Nothing pushes down -- the only step cannot be translated and there is no
shorter prefix -- yet the relation is still named in the source tables, because
the question "what does this rule read" does not depend on who answers it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("note", ABORT("no")))
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.pure-memory.unbound-source
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ROWS .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
===

### name: plan.pure-memory.not-a-pipeline
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
COUNT(ORDERS) > 0
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.tables.physical-name-not-binding-name
--- note
The whole of finding F2. `source_tables` names what the database will be asked
to read, so a caller can check grants or pick a connection; the SEL name is
what the caller already has.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"SALES": {"kind": "relation", "from": "sales_orders", "alias": "s", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "s", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
SALES .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
sales_orders
--- expect
SELECT `s`.* FROM `sales_orders` `s` LIMIT 1
===

### name: plan.tables.binding-name-is-case-insensitive
--- note
Identifiers are case-insensitive (spec §2.3), and a binding is looked up the
same way. The physical name is reported exactly as the binding spelled it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "Orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
orders .> take(1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
Orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `Orders` `o` LIMIT 1
===

### name: plan.tables.first-use-order
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> TAKE(2)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
customers
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`) LIMIT 2
===

### name: plan.tables.dedupe-by-physical-source
--- note
Two bindings over one table are one physical source. Deduplicated by the
physical name, so a self-join reads `orders` once however it is bound.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "O2": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o2", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o2", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(O2, _1["id"] == _2["id"]) .> TAKE(2)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `orders` `o2` ON (`o`.`id` = `o2`.`id`) LIMIT 2
===

### name: plan.tables.relation-query-is-reported-verbatim
--- note
A relation whose `from` is a query has no table name to report; the query text
is the physical source, and it is reported exactly as the application wrote
it, parentheses and all.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"RECENT": {"kind": "relation", "from": {"raw": "(SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id > 10)"}, "alias": "r", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "r", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
RECENT .> MAP(RECORD("note", ABORT("no")))
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
(SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id > 10)
===

### name: plan.hybrid.left-join-then-projection
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK_LEFT(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> SELECT_COLS("id")
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
customers
--- expect
SELECT "o".* FROM "orders" "o" LEFT JOIN "customers" "c" ON ("o"."customer_id" = "c"."id")
===

### name: plan.pure-sql.filters-fuse-before-planning
--- note
The logical optimiser runs before the prefix search, so two FILTERs are one
WHERE. The planner's options object reaches that optimiser as well as the
translator in every host that has optimiser options.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["id"] > 1) .> FILTER(_["id"] < 9)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE ((`o`.`id` > 1) AND (`o`.`id` < 9))
===

### name: plan.pure-sql.sort-take-fuses-to-top
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_BY(_["id"]) .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` ORDER BY `o`.`id` ASC LIMIT 1
===

### name: plan.pure-sql.filter-drop-take
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["id"] > 1) .> DROP(1) .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT "o".* FROM "orders" "o" WHERE ("o"."id" > 1) LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1
===

### name: plan.pure-sql.distinct
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> DISTINCT()
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT DISTINCT "o".* FROM "orders" "o"
===

### name: plan.options.strict-off-caveat-pushes-down
--- note
The one planner option every host documents. UPPER carries a caveat; without
strict it is translated and the whole pipeline is SQL.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(2) .> FILTER(UPPER(_["name"]) $== "A")
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `_sub1`.* FROM (SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 2) `_sub1` WHERE (CAST(UPPER(`_sub1`.`name`) AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('A' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===

### name: plan.options.strict-on-caveat-stays-in-memory
--- note
The same pipeline under strict: the caveated FILTER is refused, so the plan
splits after the TAKE. That the option reached the translator is what this
asserts; it is the same option the runner passes to translate().
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- options
{"strict": true}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(2) .> FILTER(UPPER(_["name"]) $== "A")
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 2
===

### name: plan.fold.literal-branch-is-hoisted-into-the-prefix
--- note
The planner folds before it probes, unlike translate(): a constant-condition
IF whose chosen branch is a leaf literal reaches the SQL as that literal, and
the SQL says `2`, not `CASE WHEN TRUE`.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(IF(TRUE, 2, 1) >= _["id"])
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (2 >= `o`.`id`)
===

### name: plan.fold.compound-branch-stays-an-if
--- note
The limit of that fold, and a decision: a branch with positions of its own is
not hoisted, because a continuation that runs it in memory must report errors
where spec §6.3 says (ctl.if.constant-condition-compound-branch-keeps-its-own-
positions), and the planner folds one tree for both halves. So the IF reaches
the translator, which renders it the way translate() always has. The price is
a `CASE WHEN TRUE` the database folds itself, for a shape -- a literal
condition around a column expression -- that mostly arises from a value
binding inlined by stage 1.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(IF(TRUE, _["id"] > 1, FALSE))
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE CASE WHEN TRUE THEN (`o`.`id` > 1) ELSE FALSE END
===

### name: plan.fold.hoisted-literal-does-not-change-the-split
--- note
A hoisted literal the SQL side cannot use -- `"x"` is not a number -- leaves
the MAP to the continuation, exactly as the unfolded IF would have. The
position that continuation reports when it runs is the unit tests' half of
this (each host executes the plan and compares with run()).
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(2) .> MAP(IF(TRUE, "x", 1) >= _["id"])
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 2
===

### name: plan.bucket.bare-bucket-at-the-end-is-pure-memory
--- note
Review 2026-09-15 finding I. The whole-pipeline probe consulted the
translator alone, which happily renders a bare bucket as its keys, so a
pipeline that ENDS in an open bucket was pure_sql and execute_hybrid handed
back key rows where run() answers a map of member lists. The shape guard now
covers the whole pipeline, not only the prefixes.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"])
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.bucket.open-bucket-at-the-end-splits-before-it
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["amount"] > 1) .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"])
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`amount` > 1)
===

### name: plan.bucket.sealed-bucket-at-the-end-is-pure-memory
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"]) .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.bucket.having-at-the-end-is-pure-memory
--- note
A FILTER after a bare bucket is its HAVING, but the bucket is still open at
the end: the rows would be keys.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"]) .> FILTER(COUNT(_) > 1)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.bucket.bucket-over-buckets-is-pure-memory
--- note
Finding AI from the planner's side: the translator refuses the second
bucket, every prefix ends in an open bucket, so nothing is pushed.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"]) .> BUCKET(COUNT(_)) .> MAP(RECORD("size", _K, "n", COUNT(_)))
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.aliased-pair-passes-through-by-key
--- note
Review 2026-09-15 finding P. The MAP fall-through projects the pushable pairs
in SQL and computes the custom ones in memory -- but the continuation used to
be the ORIGINAL record body re-applied to the rows that came back, so a pair
whose key is not its column name (`"cid", _["customer_id"]`) was re-read as
`_["customer_id"]` over a row that only has `cid`: E_NO_KEY. The
continuation now passes a pushable pair through by key; the SQL is unchanged
and that is what this case pins -- the executed half is a unit test per host.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("cid", _["customer_id"], "tag", ABORT("x"))) .> TAKE(3)
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`customer_id` AS `cid` FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 3
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.expression-pair-passes-through-by-key
--- note
The same for a computed pair: `_["amount"] + 1` is evaluated once, in SQL,
not again in memory over a row that already holds the sum.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("plus", _["amount"] + 1, "tag", ABORT("x"))) .> TAKE(3)
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT (`o`.`amount` + 1) AS `plus` FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 3
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.downstream-bucket-stays-in-memory
--- note
Every step after the MAP goes into the SQL, so a step that changes the row
shape -- BUCKET, another MAP, SELECT_COLS, LINK -- would put the custom half
over rows that are no longer the MAP's input. The fall-through fires only
when every downstream step keeps the rows as they are (FILTER, SORT_BY,
TOP_BY, TAKE, DROP); otherwise the split moves before the MAP, or the plan
stays in memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("customer_id", _["customer_id"], "tag", ABORT("x"))) .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"])
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.downstream-map-splits-before-the-map
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(5) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "tag", ABORT("x"))) .> MAP(RECORD("i", _["id"]))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 5
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.downstream-dedupe-splits-before-the-map
--- note
DEDUPE, DISTINCT and the keyless sorts compare whole rows, and the SQL rows
carry the custom half's dependencies where SEL's carry the custom values:
not the same comparison, so they are not pushed past the MAP either.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(5) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "tag", ABORT(_["name"]))) .> DEDUPE()
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 5
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.downstream-read-of-a-dependency-stays-in-memory
--- note
After the MAP, SEL's row has `id` and `tag`; a FILTER on `name` is E_NO_KEY.
The rewritten MAP projects `name` for the custom half, so the same FILTER
would have translated and answered. Downstream steps may read only the
pushable keys.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "tag", ABORT(_["name"]))) .> FILTER(_["name"] $== "x")
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.whole-row-read-stays-in-memory
--- note
`GET(_, "name")` reads the row itself, not a field the planner can see, so
the custom half needs the whole row and the MAP cannot be split.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "row", ABORT(GET(_, "name")))) .> TAKE(3)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.dependency-colliding-with-a-pushable-key-stays-in-memory
--- note
The custom half reads `customer_id`, and the pushable half already projects
a column under that name -- holding `amount`. Projecting the dependency
would give the continuation the wrong value under the right name.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("customer_id", _["amount"], "tag", ABORT(_["customer_id"]))) .> TAKE(3)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.downstream-read-under-its-own-binder-stays-in-memory
--- note
Cross-validation of the 2026-09-15 fix: the downstream check looked for
reads of `_` only, so a step with its own binder (`SORT_BY(s, s["name"])`)
read a dependency column unnoticed and the plan answered rows where run()
is E_NO_KEY. A field read under ANY name counts.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(r, RECORD("id", r["id"], "tag", ABORT(r["name"]))) .> SORT_BY(s, s["name"])
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.filter-under-its-own-binder-reads-a-pushable-key
--- note
The control: the same spelling reading a projected key is pushed down.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(r, RECORD("id", r["id"], "tag", ABORT(r["name"]))) .> FILTER(s, s["id"] > 1)
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`id` AS `id`, `o`.`name` AS `name` FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`id` > 1)
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.dependency-differing-from-a-key-only-by-case-stays-in-memory
--- note
SEL's record keys are case-sensitive: after `MAP(RECORD("Name", _["name"],
...))` the row has `Name`, and the custom half's `_["name"]` is a dependency
that must be projected as well. The planner compared names case-folded,
dropped the dependency as "already projected", and the continuation raised
E_NO_KEY. Names are compared exactly; and because SQL aliases are not
case-sensitive everywhere, a dependency that differs from a projected key
only by case is not projected beside it but keeps the MAP in memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("Name", _["name"], "tag", ABORT(_["name"]))) .> TAKE(2)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.custom-key-differing-from-a-pushable-key-only-by-case
--- note
And the continuation must tell the two apart by identity, not by folded
name: `x` is passed through by key, `X` is computed. One host decided by
upper-cased name and computed neither.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("x", _["id"], "X", ABORT("x"))) .> TAKE(2)
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`id` AS `x` FROM `orders` `o` LIMIT 2
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.two-dependencies-differing-only-by-case-stay-in-memory
--- note
The same rule between two dependencies: `name` and `Name` would be projected
side by side under aliases some databases cannot tell apart.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "a", ABORT(_["name"]), "b", ABORT(_["Name"]))) .> TAKE(2)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.fallthrough.case-is-ascii-case
--- note
"Not case-sensitive everywhere" is an ASCII statement, like every case rule
in SEL (UPPER, identifiers): `namé` and `NAMÉ` are two names to the planner,
not one, so the dependency is projected and the plan is hybrid. Three hosts
folded with their native upper-casing and stayed in memory while two did not.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAMé": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("NAMÉ", _["id"], "tag", ABORT(_["namé"]))) .> TAKE(2)
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT "o"."id" AS "NAMÉ", "o"."name" AS "namé" FROM "orders" "o" LIMIT 2
===

### name: plan.bucket.filter-after-pagination-is-a-where
--- note
Review 2026-09-15 finding H, planner side: the same shape every host now
renders as a derived table with the LIMIT inside and the FILTER outside,
which is what run() answers (TAKE first, then FILTER).
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"], RECORD("cid", _K, "n", COUNT(_))) .> TAKE(2) .> FILTER(_["n"] > 1)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `_sub1`.* FROM (SELECT `o`.`customer_id` AS `cid`, COUNT(*) AS `n` FROM `orders` `o` GROUP BY `o`.`customer_id` LIMIT 2) `_sub1` WHERE (CASE WHEN (`_sub1`.`n` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`_sub1`.`n` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END > 1)
===

### name: plan.bucket.filter-after-pagination-on-sqlite-splits
--- note
SQLite has no way to ask whether the derived `n` is a number, so the FILTER
is refused there and the split lands after the TAKE -- one classification
for all five hosts, where one host used to split and four pushed a wrong
HAVING.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"], RECORD("cid", _K, "n", COUNT(_))) .> TAKE(2) .> FILTER(_["n"] > 1)
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT "o"."customer_id" AS "cid", COUNT(*) AS "n" FROM "orders" "o" GROUP BY "o"."customer_id" LIMIT 2
===

### name: plan.bucket.bool-key-in-a-bare-bucket-stays-in-memory
--- note
Finding G, planner side: the evaluator refuses a boolean key in a bare
bucket (E_NOT_TEXT), so no SQL may group by one for that spelling.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ACTIVE": {"table": "o", "column": "active", "type": "BOOL"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["active"]) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _K, "n", COUNT(_)))
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.take.count-sel-refuses-stays-in-memory
--- note
Finding U from the planner's side: the refusal is a SqlError like any other,
so the plan is pure memory -- never a host exception out of plan_hybrid.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(1 / 0)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.refuse.base-dialect
--- note
Planning refuses what translate() refuses up front: a base is not a target,
whatever the pipeline turns out to be, and an alias collision is a mistake in
the bindings rather than in the rule. Neither is a classification.
--- dialect
ansi
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> TAKE(1)
--- plan
refused
--- error
E_SQL_DIALECT
===

### name: plan.immutable.folding-does-not-write-back
--- note
The whole of finding F3. The logical optimiser folds `1 + 1` and `2 * 2` on
the way to the prefix, and the caller's tree must still say `1 + 1` and
`2 * 2` afterwards: the Lisp optimiser wrote the folded literal back into the
node it was handed, which every other host copies first. A fixture whose
constants do not fold cannot see the difference, because writing a child back
unchanged leaves the same tree.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["id"] > 1 + 1) .> TAKE(2 * 2)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`id` > 2) LIMIT 4
===

### name: plan.immutable.folding-inside-a-kept-step
--- note
The same, where the folded expression sits inside the step that stays in
memory as well as the one that goes to SQL, so both the planner's copy and the
continuation are built from a tree the caller still owns.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(NOT (_["id"] > 1 + 1)) .> MAP(RECORD("n", ABORT("x")))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (NOT (`o`.`id` > 2))
===

### name: plan.bucket.pipeline-map-pushes-down-whole
--- note
The pipeline spelling of a grouped projection is one statement, so the whole
pipeline is SQL -- it used to split after the bucket and count one row per
group.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("cid", _K, "n", COUNT(_)))
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`customer_id` AS `cid`, COUNT(*) AS `n` FROM `orders` `o` GROUP BY `o`.`customer_id`
===

### name: plan.bucket.open-prefix-is-not-a-split-point
--- note
The MAP cannot be translated, and the prefix before it is a bare bucket whose
SQL rows are keys, not groups: no continuation could recover the members, so
it is not a split point and the MAP fall-through does not fire either. Pure
memory is the only right answer.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("cid", _K, "note", ABORT("x")))
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.bucket.split-before-the-bucket
--- note
The split lands before the bucket instead: the FILTER goes to the database,
the grouping and its untranslatable projection stay in memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["amount"] > 1) .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("cid", _K, "note", ABORT("x")))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`amount` > 1)
===

### name: plan.bucket.projected-then-custom-map
--- note
A bucket closed by its MAP is an ordinary row source again, so a later MAP
that cannot be translated splits after the grouping.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("cid", _K, "n", COUNT(_))) .> MAP(RECORD("c", _["cid"], "note", ABORT("x")))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`customer_id` AS `cid`, COUNT(*) AS `n` FROM `orders` `o` GROUP BY `o`.`customer_id`
===

### name: plan.bucket.sealed-prefix-is-not-a-split-point
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"]) .> TAKE(2) .> MAP(RECORD("cid", _K))
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
===

### name: plan.pure-sql.constant-true-filter
--- note
Finding E from the planner's side. A FILTER whose predicate is a constant
TRUE is kept when it is the first step over a source that might be a scalar
(conformance agg.filter.scalar-source-with-constant-predicate), and a
relation is such a source: the pipeline still has a step, and it pushes
down. Every planner used to fold the step away and classify the bare
relation as pure memory -- then execute_hybrid raised E_UNDEF_VAR for the
relation the database was supposed to answer for.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(TRUE)
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE TRUE
===

### name: plan.sort.binder-form-keeps-its-form-when-the-key-folds
--- note
Finding A from the planner's side. `O` is the binder and the IF is its key;
the planner folds constants, and a fold that hoisted "DESC" into the third
slot would turn the call into the direction form, with O an undefined
variable in the continuation. The prefix is the binder form the translator
was always handed: a constant key, ascending.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> FILTER(_["id"] > 0) .> SORT_BY(O, IF(TRUE, "DESC", "ASC"))
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` WHERE (`o`.`id` > 0) ORDER BY CAST(CASE WHEN TRUE THEN 'DESC' ELSE 'ASC' END AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin ASC
===

### name: plan.sort.direction-form-folds-to-a-literal
--- note
The other form: the second slot is a field, so the third is the direction
whatever its shape, and the planner's fold makes it the literal the
translator can read. (translate_statement, which does not fold, refuses it:
stmt.order-by.direction-that-is-not-a-literal-is-refused.)
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_BY(_["id"], IF(TRUE, "DESC", "ASC"))
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` ORDER BY `o`.`id` DESC
===

### name: plan.map.computed-field-then-keyless-sort-stays-after-the-map
--- note
Finding C2 from the planner's side: the MAP/SORT swap may fire only for a
key over fields the MAP passes through. A keyless sort compares the MAP's
outputs -- here the negated quantity -- and every planner used to sort the
source instead, in the opposite order.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("q", 0 - _["amount"])) .> SORT()
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `_sub1`.* FROM (SELECT (0 - `o`.`amount`) AS `q` FROM `orders` `o`) `_sub1` ORDER BY `_sub1`.`q` ASC
===

### name: plan.map.pass-through-key-sorts-early
--- note
The shape the swap is for: the key reads only a field the MAP passes
through, so the sort runs on the source and the projection is rendered
over the sorted rows in one statement.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "q", 0 - _["amount"])) .> SORT_BY(_["id"], "DESC")
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`id` AS `id`, (0 - `o`.`amount`) AS `q` FROM `orders` `o` ORDER BY `o`.`id` DESC
===

### name: plan.sort.then-filter-on-the-key-splits
--- note
A FILTER that reads `_K` after a sort reads the sorted position; moving it
in front of the sort would read the source's keys. The sort is the prefix,
the FILTER the continuation -- four planners swapped the two and, with the
FILTER then untranslatable, classified the whole pipeline as pure memory.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> SORT_BY(_["id"]) .> FILTER(_K == "1")
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
--- expect
SELECT `o`.* FROM `orders` `o` ORDER BY `o`.`id` ASC
===
